WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.000
Fieldnote six. The handshake.

2
00:00:08.852 --> 00:00:11.252
How does a candidate image
reach a running stack,

3
00:00:11.252 --> 00:00:15.582
what proves it, and how is the
temporary test deployment restored?

4
00:00:17.164 --> 00:00:20.774
Write the lock. Flux rolls out the image.

5
00:00:21.084 --> 00:00:23.574
The stack reports its configuration.

6
00:00:23.964 --> 00:00:28.214
The fork's descriptor lists its
test suites and their inputs.

7
00:00:28.524 --> 00:00:31.414
And the lock is the only thing both write.

8
00:00:33.196 --> 00:00:35.366
Writing the lock starts the deploy.

9
00:00:35.676 --> 00:00:39.596
The run writes the candidate
digest, and its own run ID,

10
00:00:39.596 --> 00:00:43.036
into osdu-image-lock.

11
00:00:43.036 --> 00:00:47.766
Flux reconciles the lock, and the
pod restarts on the pinned digest.

12
00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:51.766
The run checks the pod before
it trusts any test result.

13
00:00:52.236 --> 00:00:55.606
Between runs, the lock
holds the canonical image.

14
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.960
A green restore is a claim about this run,

15
00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.810
not about the environment.

16
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.090
The run only restores what it still owns.

17
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:11.290
Reset writes the canonical image back
only while the lock still names this run.

18
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.090
If a newer run has pinned the slot,
it writes nothing and exits 2,

19
00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.410
and the lane counts that as success.

20
00:01:20.896 --> 00:01:24.426
The stack publishes facts,
and the fork declares needs.

21
00:01:25.056 --> 00:01:29.946
spi status and spi info report
the stack's configuration.

22
00:01:30.416 --> 00:01:34.666
The fork's service descriptor
declares its suites and their inputs.

23
00:01:35.136 --> 00:01:39.546
Five repository settings, plus
trust onboarding on the stack side,

24
00:01:39.616 --> 00:01:41.066
are all that connect them.

25
00:01:43.332 --> 00:01:44.702
Follow candidate B.

26
00:01:44.972 --> 00:01:46.382
Validation pushes it.

27
00:01:46.692 --> 00:01:49.582
The gate decides whether
this run may borrow.

28
00:01:49.972 --> 00:01:53.982
The pin writes the lock, from
A to B, owned by this run.

29
00:01:54.292 --> 00:01:57.502
Verify waits until the
pod's image ID is B.

30
00:01:57.652 --> 00:02:01.662
Prove runs the suites the descriptor
declares and keeps their reports.

31
00:02:01.812 --> 00:02:04.942
And restore writes the
lock back from B to A,

32
00:02:05.092 --> 00:02:06.702
if this run still owns it.

33
00:02:07.092 --> 00:02:09.982
Every step leaves
something you can inspect.

34
00:02:12.176 --> 00:02:13.256
An easy mistake:

35
00:02:13.256 --> 00:02:17.586
Validation Summary is green, so the
change ran in a real environment.

36
00:02:17.816 --> 00:02:20.936
The deploy lane can skip,
with a visible reason,

37
00:02:20.936 --> 00:02:22.786
and the summary still passes.

38
00:02:23.016 --> 00:02:25.346
The repository may not be onboarded.

39
00:02:25.416 --> 00:02:27.106
There may be no descriptor.

40
00:02:27.336 --> 00:02:31.426
No image was pushed, or the run
came from another repository.

41
00:02:31.576 --> 00:02:33.506
The Deploy Gate says which.

42
00:02:33.736 --> 00:02:36.786
To check, read the Deploy
Gate's log for the skip.

43
00:02:39.068 --> 00:02:40.428
So now you can say:

44
00:02:40.428 --> 00:02:42.678
writing the lock starts the deploy.

45
00:02:43.228 --> 00:02:45.878
A green restore is a claim about this run.

46
00:02:46.188 --> 00:02:50.438
And the stack publishes facts,
while the fork declares needs.

47
00:02:52.694 --> 00:02:56.494
Next: which reasonable assumptions
will cost me an afternoon?

